      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Starting the rankings requires evaluating each zone’s immediate vulnerability to prolonged insecurity combined with their socioeconomic capacity to participate in stabilization efforts. Zones with higher disease rates and lower income levels often show a better ROI for emergency kits because targeted interventions can prevent cascading health failures that complicate long-term recovery. Conversely, prioritizing areas with strong community infrastructure first might save costs, but only if those areas face imminent threats that their existing resources cannot absorb.\n\nPlease provide the specific demographics and socioeconomic data for the zones so I can calculate the optimal distribution order to maximize both speed and stability.",
